  "findings": [
    "The 220 Hz Bfxr input descriptor measures approximately 220.5 Hz; feature normalization produces ordinary magnitudes. Its large raw-head pitch error is not explained by losing the target fundamental or a gross normalization mismatch.",
    "The Bfxr dataset contains no stationary sine examples under the explicit pitch-motion criterion, despite 255 examples passing that criterion overall. The sine category has only 80 of 2048 examples. Engine/generator balancing does not imply acoustic category or control-interaction balancing.",
    "The Bfxr validation objective is dominated by classification; generator and waveform classification contribute about 82 percent of weighted loss. Thirty continuous sliders share the remaining unweighted MSE, including controls that may be acoustically inactive.",
    "Generator-conditioned predictions for the same 220 Hz audio vary substantially. Generator ancestry is a weak or ambiguous acoustic label after global mutation and can impose inappropriate control/category priors.",
    "More epochs already overfit the existing distribution: best validation is epoch 12, while the epoch 50 training loss is lower and validation loss is higher.",
    "The pitch descriptor uses integer autocorrelation lags without window-autocorrelation correction or sub-sample interpolation. Around 880 Hz it can introduce roughly 0.6\u20130.75 semitone error; this merits a later independently validated feature revision but cannot explain the 220 Hz raw-head error."
  ],
  "recommendedNextLearnedExperiment": {
    "priority": "Add independent structured synthetic coverage and improve the supervised acoustic loss before extending training epochs.",
    "data": "Add a broad log-uniform pitch curriculum across actual waveform/voice categories, with stationary tones, sparse pitch ramps/jumps, varied durations/envelopes and near-zero-motion examples. Preserve the original generator/global draws. Reserve fresh pitches, control combinations and render seeds for evaluation; do not derive labels or controls from user-rated target audio.",
    "loss": "Mask acoustically inactive controls and measure per-engine control observability with the actual renderer. Weight audible pitch/temporal controls accordingly; use log-frequency error for controls whose DSP mapping is known, so octave errors are represented directly rather than diluted among linear slider errors.",
    "conditioning": "Ablate hard generator conditioning for numeric/category heads against conditioning on physically meaningful discrete voice/waveform choices; keep generator identity recorded and evaluate generator classification separately.",
    "precedent": "The existing invert.dataset stratifies waveform types in its random slice and includes a 25 percent structured tonal slice. Existing invert.train masks square-only Bfxr controls, while invert.constants contains actual-renderer sensitivity weights.",
    "comparison": "Compare structured-data-only and loss-only ablations against the frozen v1 checkpoint, using the same fresh held-out reconstruction and tonal protocols. No inference-time pitch correction should be presented as a learned gain."
  }
